<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mashobra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mashobra is a hill resort about 12 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, set among forests of oak and pine at more than 2,000 metres. In the colonial period it lay among the small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simla-hill-states">Simla Hill States</a> and became a summer retreat for British officials. The Retreat at Mashobra, built in the 19th century, is now a summer residence of the President of India.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mashobra.jpg" alt="Mashobra" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Supreet · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mashobra_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mashobra is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a> district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a>, at an average elevation of 2,146 metres, linked to Shimla by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindustan">Hindustan</a>–Tibet Road, built in 1850 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-dalhousie">Lord Dalhousie</a>. It lies within the Shimla Water Catchment and Wildlife Sanctuary, where pine, oak, Himalayan deodar, rhododendron, maple and horse chestnut grow, and where monkeys, langurs, jackals, barking deer, the occasional leopard and birds such as the Himalayan eagle, pheasants, chikor and partridges live.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the colonial period Mashobra belonged to the princely state of Koti. Towards the end of the 19th century it became a popular weekend resort for the elite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a>, the summer capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a>. Wildflower Hall at nearby Chharabra was a residence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-kitchener">Lord Kitchener</a> and of Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ripon">Ripon</a>. About 3 km from Mashobra, the Italian photographer Federico Peliti built a villa he named Carignano after his native town near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>; it became a weekend resort in 1920.</p><p>In May 1948, before returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> at the end of his mission as viceroy and then governor general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-mountbatten">Lord Mountbatten</a> spent a few weeks at the retreat with his wife, Lady Edwina, and Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> visited them there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The retreat, a wooden building first constructed in 1850, is one of the two presidential retreats of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the other being Rashtrapati Nilayam in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secunderabad">Secunderabad</a>, and the president visits it at least once a year. Wildflower Hall is a property of Oberoi Hotels, and the Himalayan International School at Chharabra is a large residential school.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mashobra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Koti was a small mountain state near <b>Mashobra</b> and was a feudatory of Keonthal State.</blockquote><a href="/state/koti-state">Koti State</a></li></ul></section>
